xmlChar *
xmlParsePubidLiteral(xmlParserCtxt *ctxt) {
    xmlChar *buf = NULL;
    int len = 0;
    int size = XML_PARSER_BUFFER_SIZE;  // 100
    int maxLength = (ctxt->options & XML_PARSE_HUGE) ?
                    XML_MAX_TEXT_LENGTH :
                    XML_MAX_NAME_LENGTH;
    xmlChar cur;
    xmlChar stop;

    if (RAW == '"') {
        NEXT;
        stop = '"';
    } else if (RAW == '\'') {
        NEXT;
        stop = '\'';
    } else {
        xmlFatalErr(ctxt, XML_ERR_LITERAL_NOT_STARTED, NULL);
        return(NULL);
    }
    buf = xmlMalloc(size);  // allocates 100 bytes
    if (buf == NULL) {
        xmlErrMemory(ctxt);
        return(NULL);
    }
    cur = CUR;
    while ((IS_PUBIDCHAR_CH(cur)) && (cur != stop) &&
           (PARSER_STOPPED(ctxt) == 0)) { /* checked */
        if (len + 1 >= size) {  // when len+1 >= 100, i.e., len >= 99
            xmlChar *tmp;
            int newSize;

            newSize = xmlGrowCapacity(size, 1, 1, maxLength);  // grows size
            if (newSize < 0) {
                xmlFatalErr(ctxt, XML_ERR_NAME_TOO_LONG, "Public ID");
                xmlFree(buf);
                return(NULL);
            }
            tmp = xmlRealloc(buf, size);  // BUG: reallocs to OLD size, not newSize!
            if (tmp == NULL) {
                xmlErrMemory(ctxt);
                xmlFree(buf);
                return(NULL);
            }
            buf = tmp;
            size = newSize;  // size is updated to newSize (larger)
        }
        buf[len++] = cur;  // writes at index len
        NEXT;
        cur = CUR;
    }
    buf[len] = 0;  // writes null terminator at index len
    ...
}
```

The bug is on line 4449: `tmp = xmlRealloc(buf, size);` — it reallocates using the OLD `size` (100), but then sets `size = newSize` (the grown size). So the buffer is NOT actually grown. The realloc just reallocates the same size.

Wait, let me re-read. The realloc uses `size` which is still the old value (100) at the point of the realloc call. Then `size = newSize` updates size. So the buffer remains at 100 bytes.

So when `len` reaches 99, `len + 1 >= size` (100) is true. We enter the block. `newSize = xmlGrowCapacity(100, 1, 1, maxLength)`. Since capacity (100) > 0, and assuming 100 < maxLength, extra = (100+1)/2 = 50, so newSize = 150. Then `tmp = xmlRealloc(buf, size)` where size is still 100. So buf is reallocated to 100 bytes (same size). Then `size = newSize` = 150.

Now back in the loop: `buf[len++] = cur` writes at index 99 (len was 99, then becomes 100). That's within the 100-byte buffer (index 99 is the last valid byte). 

